1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,998
Book Three, Proposition Twenty-Eight.

2
00:00:02,998 --> 00:00:10,732
In equal circles, equal segments cut off equal arcs, the greater equal to the greater and the less to the less.

3
00:00:10,732 --> 00:00:20,975
Let A B C and D E F be equal circles, and let equal chords A B and D E cut off A C B and D F E as greater arcs, and A G B and D H E as lesser arcs.

4
00:00:20,975 --> 00:00:26,410
We must prove that arc A C B equals arc D F E, and arc A G B equals arc D H E.

5
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:34,000
Take the centers K and L of the two circles, and join the radii A K, K B, D L, and L E.

6
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:39,868
Because the circles are equal, their radii A K, K B, D L, and L E are all equal.

7
00:00:39,868 --> 00:00:52,261
And since the base A B equals the base D E, the three sides of triangle A K B equal the three sides of triangle D L E, so central angle A K B equals central angle D L E by One Eight.

8
00:00:52,261 --> 00:01:00,569
By Three Twenty-Six, equal angles at the centers stand on equal arcs, so the lesser arc A G B equals the lesser arc D H E.

9
00:01:00,569 --> 00:01:13,780
And since the whole circle A B C equals the whole circle D E F, subtracting the equal lesser arcs leaves the remaining greater arc A C B equal to the greater arc D F E, as was required to prove.

10
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:19,311
Proposition Twenty-Eight translates linear equality of chords into curved equality of arcs.

11
00:01:19,311 --> 00:01:32,511
As Heath observes, if one of the equal chords passes through the center as a diameter, the other chord must also be a diameter by Three Fifteen, since the diameter is the greatest chord in a circle; thus both cut off equal semicircles.

12
00:01:32,511 --> 00:01:45,880
